all right so this talk is going to be  asynchronous because it's going to  execute out of order it's going to be  something like when should I use it how  does it work and then by the time you've  seen how it works you'll kind of  understand what it is so my name is  Jesse Davis I work for MongoDB here in  New York City  hi guys yeah as you may know in New York  there's three kinds of food so we have  sub sandwiches pizza by the slice and  omakase which is a word meaning  something like chef's choice it's when  you go into a sushi restaurant and they  give you whatever is good that night so  New York is a crowded place and there's  a lot of scaling difficulties with  serving all the people but each kind of  food has a different scaling challenge  so first of all when you want to get a  sub you go to the counter and a sandwich  maker comes out of the sandwich maker  pool and you both go to the counter and  you tell the sandwich maker what kind of  sandwich you want what ingredients and  you have this synchronous one-on-one  interaction which lasts for the duration  of the entire sandwich making process  the sandwich maker doesn't talk to a  different client doesn't take a break  that person is completely dedicated to  you until the sandwich is done and you  walk away with it at which point the  sandwich maker returns to the pool and  then maybe takes the next client off of  the eater queue so this is sort of like  a  cpu-bound web service where each client  requests requires a lot of computation  but no external resources in order to  satisfy it and in that case you should  have say a thread or a process per  request which handles the entire request  from start to finish before it begins on  another request so this is an example of  the kind of application that does not  benefit from async because there's no  idle time during the course of  completing a client request just use  multiprocessing to handle this problem  there's no advantage to concurrency  whatsoever bu